[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Thinking: The user wants to add a satellite imagery tile service as bottom layer. Provide instructions: create raster source, add raster layer, set before other layers, ensure CORS, use style JSON or addSource and addLayer. Mention tileSize default 256, but for JPEG need raster source type, not raster-dem. Provide example code. Also discuss attribution, maxzoom, etc. Provide full HTML/JS snippet. Also note need to set crossOrigin if needed. Provide steps.

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Create a `raster` source** that points at your JPEG tiles (`256 px` each).  
2. **Add a raster layer** that uses that source and is placed at the bottom of the style (so everything else draws on top of it).  
3. (Optional) add attribution, set a sensible `maxzoom`, and make sure CORS is allowed.

> **Important:**  
> * JPEG tiles are **not** a `raster-dem` source – they are ordinary imagery, so we use `type: "raster"`.  
> * The default `tileSize` for a raster source is **256**, which matches your tiles, so you don’t have to set it explicitly (but you can if you want to be explicit).  
> * The tile server must send the proper **CORS** header (`Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` or your domain) otherwise the browser will block the request.

---

## 1️⃣ Minimal HTML page

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Satellite imagery base layer</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',               // id of the div above
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // A very small “empty” style – we’ll add our own sources/layers later
      sources: {},
      layers: []
    },
    center: [0, 0],                 // lon, lat
    zoom: 2,
    // If you have a token for a different provider (e.g. MapTiler) you could
    // also start from their style URL and then add the raster source on top.
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Add the raster source that points at your JPEG tiles
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('sat-imagery', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: [
        // <-- replace with your own domain if you need a different sub‑domain
        'https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,          // optional – default is 256, but makes intent clear
      // Optional but recommended:
      maxzoom: 19,            // highest zoom level your tiles actually exist at
      attribution: '© Example Satellite Imagery',
      // If your server does NOT send CORS headers you can try:
      //   crossOrigin: 'anonymous',
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Add a raster layer that uses the source
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sat-imagery',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'sat-imagery',
      // The raster layer is drawn *below* any other layers that already exist.
      // Because we started from an empty style, this will be the bottommost layer.
      // If you later add other layers (e.g. vector roads, labels) they will appear on top.
      paint: {
        // Optional: control opacity (1 = fully opaque)
        'raster-opacity': 1.0
      }
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ (Optional) Add a simple vector layer on top to prove ordering
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Example: a world‑countries outline from MapLibre's demo tiles
    map.addSource('countries', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/tiles.json' // vector tileset
    });
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-outline',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'countries',
      'source-layer': 'countries',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#fff',
        'line-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Section | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| **`map.addSource('sat-imagery', …)`** | Declares a **raster** source that fetches JPEG tiles from `https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`. |
| **`tileSize: 256`** | Explicitly tells MapLibre the tiles are 256 px. (If omitted, 256 is the default.) |
| **`maxzoom`** | Prevents MapLibre from requesting zoom levels that don’t exist (you can set it to the highest zoom you actually have). |
| **`attribution`** | Shown in the default map control (if you add one) and is good practice for licensing. |
| **`map.addLayer({ … })`** | Creates a **raster** layer that draws the imagery. Because we add it right after the map loads and before any other layers, it becomes the **bottom layer**. |
| **`raster-opacity`** | Lets you make the imagery semi‑transparent if you ever need to blend it with something else. |
| **Optional vector layer** | Demonstrates that any later `addLayer` calls will appear **on top** of the satellite imagery. |

---

## 2️⃣ Using a **style JSON** instead of an inline style

If you prefer to keep the style in a separate JSON file (e.g. `style.json`), you can do:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "sat-imagery": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 19,
      "attribution": "© Example Satellite Imagery"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "sat-imagery",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "sat-imagery",
      "paint": { "raster-opacity": 1.0 }
    }
    // …any other layers you want to add later…
  ]
}
```

Then initialise the map with:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style.json',   // URL to the JSON above
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Tiles don’t appear, console shows CORS errors** | The tile server isn’t sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. | Add the header on the server side (e.g., `*` or your domain). If you control the server, enable CORS. If you can’t change the server, you can proxy the tiles through a server you control that adds the header. |
| **Only a gray/blank background shows** | Wrong URL template (`{z}/{x}/{y}` order) or missing file extension. | Verify the URL works directly in the browser (e.g., `https://tiles.example.com/imagery/0/0/0.jpg`). |
| **Map is blurry at high zoom** | Tiles are only available up to a lower `maxzoom` than you’re requesting. | Set `maxzoom` to the highest zoom you actually have, or provide higher‑resolution tiles. |
| **Imagery is offset by a few pixels** | The tile server uses a different tile scheme (`tms` vs `xyz`). | Add `"scheme": "tms"` to the source definition if the server follows the TMS scheme. |
| **You want the imagery to be semi‑transparent** | Opacity is always 1.0. | Change `'raster-opacity': 0.6` (or any value between 0 and 1) in the layer’s `paint` property. |

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist before you go live

1. **CORS** – Confirm the tile server returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (or proxy it).  
2. **Tile size** – 256 px (default) matches your tiles; if you ever switch to 512 px, set `tileSize: 512`.  
3. **Zoom range** – Set `maxzoom` (and optionally `minzoom`) to the range you actually have.  
4. **Attribution** – Add the provider’s required attribution string.  
5. **HTTPS** – The tile URLs must be HTTPS when the page is served over HTTPS (otherwise the browser blocks mixed‑content requests).  

---

### 🎉 That’s it!

With the code above you’ll see your satellite imagery as the **bottom layer** of the map, and you can freely add any number of vector or raster layers on top (roads, labels, hillshade, 3‑D terrain, etc.). If you run into any specific error messages, drop them here and I’ll help you troubleshoot further. Happy mapping!
